1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:09,801
This video tries every trigger on one bouncing ball. A trigger decides when an animation plays by itself, and what it does when you look away.

2
00:00:10,333 --> 00:00:13,416
Let's start with the four ways to start, in a list.

3
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,543
Load, mouse over, click, and none.

4
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:28,267
Now one ball for each, labelled with its start.

5
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:56,753
Start, set on each component, replaces the start saved in the file. Only the first one plays right away, because it starts on load.

6
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,952
Let's point at the second one, and move away.

7
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:08,912
It plays while the pointer is over it, and pauses when the pointer leaves.

8
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:12,667
Now let's click the third one, twice.

9
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,235
The first click plays it, and the second one pauses it.

10
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:24,359
The last one has no trigger at all. Let's point at it, and click it.

11
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:35,097
None never plays by itself. It waits for your code, with play, progress, or the player commands.

12
00:01:36,333 --> 00:01:42,368
Mouse over has a second half, what happens when the pointer leaves. Let's list the four choices.

13
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:54,299
Pause, reset, reverse, and continue.

14
00:01:55,533 --> 00:01:59,817
Now four balls that start on mouse over, one for each choice.

15
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:25,502
The timeline property sets both halves of the trigger, the start and the leave.

16
00:02:26,733 --> 00:02:29,685
Let's point at each one, and move away.

17
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:47,754
Pause stops where it is. Reset jumps back to the start. Reverse plays back to the start, and continue keeps going.

18
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,553
Scrolling away has its own three choices. Let's list them.

19
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:01,504
Pause, continue, and reset.

20
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:06,730
Now three balls, one for each, with room to scroll below them.

21
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:31,992
Off screen says what happens while the ball cannot be seen. They all start on load, as the file says.

22
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:35,715
Let's scroll them away, and back.

23
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:49,033
Pause picks up where it stopped. Continue kept going while it was hidden, and reset starts over.

24
00:03:50,267 --> 00:03:58,913
So, a trigger is two decisions. When it starts, on load, mouse over, click, or none, and what it does when you look away.
